// One sample out of a scanline, at any of the depths a PNG may use.
//
// At 8 bits a sample is a byte. Below 8 -- 1, 2 or 4, which the format allows
// only for grey and indexed images, both single-channel -- samples are packed
// several to a byte, most significant first, and rows are padded to a byte
// boundary. That padding is why png_st[8] rounds up: a 17-pixel 4-bit row is nine
// bytes, not eight and a half.
//
// One expression covers both, and it has to. The obvious shape -- read the byte
// the 8-bit way, then correct it if the depth is smaller -- reads that byte
// *unconditionally*, and at 4 bits the 8-bit address for the last pixel of a row
// is seven bytes past the end of the image. An out-of-range store read aborts the
// process (NAMES.md 6), so the correction never runs.
//
// Where the arithmetic degenerates at depth 8: `x * 8 * n / 8` is `x * n`, the
// shift `8 - 8 - x * 8 % 8` is zero, and the mask is 256. The general case *is*
// the special case, which is the only reason one line is honest here.

png_samp(int r, int x) {
  int d = (import png_st)[2];
  int by = peek8(r + 1 + x * d * (import png_st)[4] / 8);
} return int by / inf_pow2(8 - d - x * d % 8) % inf_pow2(d);
